 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Three groups for saying «maybe»",
     "texto": [
      "There are three groups of expressions:\n\n- Almost always **indicative**: **a lo mejor**, **igual**, **seguro que**, **supongo que**. Example: **a lo mejor me quedo** at home.\n- Always **subjunctive**: **puede que**, **es posible que**, **es probable que**. Example: **puede que nieve**.\n- **Both moods**: **quizá(s)**, **tal vez**, **probablemente**, **seguramente**. Example: **quizá viene** or **quizá venga**.\n\nWith the last group, the subjunctive sounds more doubtful and the indicative more certain.\n\n**No sé si** takes the indicative in Spain: **no sé si viene**. Without an accent, **si** means «whether»; with an accent, **sí** is «yes»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Indicative, subjunctive or both",
     "texto": [
      "Follow this guide:\n\n- **Indicative**: a lo mejor · igual · seguro que · supongo que · no sé si.\n- **Subjunctive**: puede que · es posible que · es probable que.\n- **Both moods**: quizá(s) · tal vez · probablemente · posiblemente · seguramente.\n\nWith the third group, choose according to how **sure** you are: if you think it is very likely, indicative (**quizá viene**); if you doubt more, subjunctive (**quizá venga**).\n\nIf you put the adverb **after** the verb, it is always indicative: viene, **quizá**."
     ],
